## 3. Equity Metrics: DeLong Variance and Non-Inferiority Testing

### 3.1 DeLong’s Non-Parametric AUC Variance

Let \(\hat{\theta}\) be the AUC estimate from a diagnostic test. Define the placement values

\[
V_{Ai}
=
\frac{1}{n}
\sum_{j=1}^{n}
\psi(a_i, b_j),
\qquad
V_{Bj}
=
\frac{1}{m}
\sum_{i=1}^{m}
\psi(a_i, b_j),
\]

where

\[
\psi(a,b)
=
\mathbf{1}\{a > b\}
+
\frac{1}{2}\mathbf{1}\{a = b\}
\]

is the Mann–Whitney kernel. Then

\[
\operatorname{Var}[\hat{\theta}]
=
\frac{\operatorname{Var}[V_A]}{m}
+
\frac{\operatorname{Var}[V_B]}{n}.
\]

For two correlated AUCs from the same data,

\[
\operatorname{Var}[\hat{\theta}^{\alpha} - \hat{\theta}^{\beta}]
=
\operatorname{Var}[\hat{\theta}^{\alpha}]
+
\operatorname{Var}[\hat{\theta}^{\beta}]
-
2\operatorname{Cov}[\hat{\theta}^{\alpha}, \hat{\theta}^{\beta}],
\]

with

\[
\operatorname{Cov}[\hat{\theta}^{\alpha}, \hat{\theta}^{\beta}]
=
\frac{\operatorname{Cov}[V_A^{\alpha}, V_A^{\beta}]}{m}
+
\frac{\operatorname{Cov}[V_B^{\alpha}, V_B^{\beta}]}{n}.
\]

Under the null,

\[
Z
=
\frac{\hat{\theta}^{\alpha} - \hat{\theta}^{\beta}}
{\sqrt{\operatorname{Var}[\hat{\theta}^{\alpha} - \hat{\theta}^{\beta}]}}
\sim \mathcal{N}(0,1).
\]

### 3.2 Equity as a Non-Inferiority Problem

The \(\delta\)-equity criterion

\[
\max_{A,B \in \text{Ancestries}}
|\text{AUC}_A - \text{AUC}_B|
\leq \delta
\]

can be reformulated as a one-sided non-inferiority test:

\[
H_0:
\text{AUC}_{\text{disadvantaged}}
<
\text{AUC}_{\text{advantaged}} - \delta,
\]

\[
H_1:
\text{AUC}_{\text{disadvantaged}}
\geq
\text{AUC}_{\text{advantaged}} - \delta.
\]

The test statistic is

\[
Z_{\text{NI}}
=
\frac{
(\text{AUC}_1 - \text{AUC}_2) - \delta
}{
\operatorname{SE}(\text{AUC}_1 - \text{AUC}_2)
}.
\]

We reject \(H_0\) and conclude that equity holds if

\[
Z_{\text{NI}} < -z_{1-\alpha}^{\text{one-sided}}.
\]

For a two-sided \(0.05\) procedure, use \(\alpha = 0.025\) one-sided. Non-significance alone does not imply equivalence; the non-inferiority formulation is therefore mathematically cleaner and more defensible.

### 3.3 Delta Method Alternative

If \(\theta = g(\boldsymbol{\pi})\), where \(\boldsymbol{\pi}\) is the vector of confusion-matrix probabilities, then

\[
\operatorname{Var}[\hat{\theta}]
\approx
\nabla g(\hat{\boldsymbol{\pi}})^{\mathsf{T}}
\Sigma_{\boldsymbol{\pi}}
\nabla g(\hat{\boldsymbol{\pi}}),
\]

where \(\Sigma_{\boldsymbol{\pi}}\) is the covariance matrix of \(\hat{\boldsymbol{\pi}}\). This approach can address computational and mapping limitations of DeLong’s method, especially when AUC is embedded in a larger risk–benefit decision model.

