## 7. Admixed Populations: Local Ancestry and Cross-Ancestry Models

### 7.1 Local Ancestry-Aware LMM

For an admixed individual \(i\) at SNP \(j\), let \(a_{ij} \in \{1,\dots,k\}\) be the local ancestry. The ancestry-specific effect model is

\[
y_i
=
\mu
+
\sum_{a=1}^{k}
\beta_{ja}
\mathbf{1}\{a_{ij} = a\}
g_{ij}
+
\mathbf{z}_i^{\mathsf{T}}\boldsymbol{\gamma}
+
\epsilon_i,
\]

where \(\beta_{ja}\) is the effect size of SNP \(j\) in ancestry \(a\). Tractor-Mix extends this to related individuals by incorporating a random effect with covariance matrix \(\mathbf{K}\), yielding a local-ancestry-informed LMM that produces well-calibrated association studies in admixed samples with high degrees of relatedness.

### 7.2 Cross-Ancestry Genetic Correlation

The Logica framework uses a bivariate linear mixed model:

\[
\begin{pmatrix}
\mathbf{y}_1 \\
\mathbf{y}_2
\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
\mathbf{X}_1 & \mathbf{0} \\
\mathbf{0} & \mathbf{X}_2
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
\boldsymbol{\beta}_1 \\
\boldsymbol{\beta}_2
\end{pmatrix}
+
\begin{pmatrix}
\mathbf{g}_1 \\
\mathbf{g}_2
\end{pmatrix}
+
\begin{pmatrix}
\boldsymbol{\epsilon}_1 \\
\boldsymbol{\epsilon}_2
\end{pmatrix},
\]

with

\[
\begin{pmatrix}
\mathbf{g}_1 \\
\mathbf{g}_2
\end{pmatrix}
\sim
\mathcal{N}
\left(
\mathbf{0},
\begin{pmatrix}
\sigma_{g_1}^2 \mathbf{K}_1 &
\rho_g \sigma_{g_1}\sigma_{g_2} \mathbf{K}_{12} \\
\rho_g \sigma_{g_1}\sigma_{g_2} \mathbf{K}_{12}^{\mathsf{T}} &
\sigma_{g_2}^2 \mathbf{K}_2
\end{pmatrix}
\right).
\]

This explicitly models diverse LD patterns across ancestries and achieves \(2.23\)–\(4.13\times\) lower MSE for local genetic correlation estimation than method-of-moments approaches.

### 7.3 Local Ancestry Estimation Error

Local ancestry is typically estimated by methods such as RFMix, LAMP-LD, or MOSAIC. Estimation error propagates into \(\beta_{ja}\). If \(\hat{a}_{ij}\) is the estimated local ancestry and \(a_{ij}\) is the true local ancestry, then the fitted model uses

\[
\mathbf{1}\{\hat{a}_{ij} = a\}
=
\mathbf{1}\{a_{ij} = a\}
+
e_{ij},
\]

where \(e_{ij}\) is a measurement error. Switch errors and phasing errors can bias ancestry-specific effect estimates by \(10\)–\(30\%\) in highly admixed samples, such as African Americans with recent admixture. This attenuation bias should be addressed by measurement-error correction, sensitivity analysis over local ancestry uncertainty, or simulation-based calibration. Without such corrections, ancestry-specific effect estimates may appear more precise than the underlying data warrant.

