## Appendix E: Stratified FDR, HMP Calibration, and IHW

### E.1 Within-Stratum BH

For ancestry stratum \(a\) with \(m_a\) tests, the BH procedure at level \(\alpha\) controls
\[
\text{FDR}_a
=
\mathbb{E}
\left[
\frac{
|\mathcal{R}_a \cap \mathcal{H}_{0,a}|
}{
|\mathcal{R}_a| \vee 1
}
\right]
\leq \alpha,
\]
provided the p-values satisfy positive regression dependence on subsets. Within a single ancestry stratum, local LD structure typically induces this condition.

### E.2 Harmonic Mean P-Values

The harmonic mean p-value is
\[
\text{HMP}
=
\frac{k}{\sum_{a=1}^{k} \frac{1}{p_a}}.
\]
The claim \(k \cdot \text{HMP} \sim \text{Uniform}(0,1)\) asymptotically is not generally valid except for special cases such as \(k=1\). Under the null, calibration requires the exact null distribution of the HMP or a permutation/empirical null.

Permutation calibration:
1. Generate \(B\) permutation replicates of the ancestry labels.
2. Recompute the HMP for each replicate.
3. Estimate the null distribution \(\hat{F}_{\text{HMP}}\).
4. Compute calibrated p-values as
\[
p_{\text{cal}}
=
\frac{1 + \#\{b : \text{HMP}_b \leq \text{HMP}_{\text{obs}}\}}{B+1}.
\]

### E.3 Independent Hypothesis Weighting

When ancestry-specific heritability, MAF distribution, or functional annotation is informative about the prior probability of association, independent hypothesis weighting can increase power relative to BH-within-strata while maintaining FDR control. This permits underpowered ancestries to be up-weighted without inflating the global false discovery rate.

