## Appendix G: AUC Power Analysis

### G.1 Variance of AUC Difference

For correlated AUCs,
\[
\operatorname{Var}(\Delta \widehat{\text{AUC}})
=
\operatorname{Var}(\widehat{\text{AUC}}_1)
+
\operatorname{Var}(\widehat{\text{AUC}}_2)
-
2\rho
\sqrt{
\operatorname{Var}(\widehat{\text{AUC}}_1)
\operatorname{Var}(\widehat{\text{AUC}}_2)
}.
\]
The required sample size is
\[
n
=
\frac{
(z_{1-\alpha/2} + z_{1-\beta})^2
\operatorname{Var}(\Delta \widehat{\text{AUC}})
}{
\Delta^2
}.
\]
For equal variances \(\sigma^2\) and correlation \(\rho\),
\[
n
=
\frac{
2(z_{1-\alpha/2} + z_{1-\beta})^2
\sigma^2(1-\rho)
}{
\Delta^2
}.
\]

### G.2 Hanley–McNeil Limitation

The Hanley–McNeil approximation is
\[
\operatorname{Var}(\widehat{\text{AUC}})
\approx
\frac{
\text{AUC}(1-\text{AUC})
}{
\min(n_{\text{cases}}, n_{\text{controls}})
}.
\]
It underestimates variance when AUC is near \(0.5\) or near \(1.0\), and when case/control ratios are extreme. Equity studies often operate in this regime. Therefore, Hanley–McNeil-based sample sizes should be treated as lower bounds. Use Obuchowski or DeLong exact variance when precision is critical.

### G.3 Minimal Detectable Effect

For fixed \(n\),
\[
\text{MDE}
=
(z_{1-\alpha/2} + z_{1-\beta})
\sqrt{
\frac{
\operatorname{Var}(\Delta \widehat{\text{AUC}})
}{
n
}
}.
\]

