## Appendix H: Admixed Populations and Local Ancestry Error

### H.1 Local Ancestry Model

For admixed individual \(i\) at SNP \(j\), let \(a_{ij} \in \{1,\dots,k\}\) be the local ancestry. The ancestry-specific effect model is
\[
y_i
=
\mu
+
\sum_{a=1}^{k}
\beta_{ja}
\mathbf{1}\{a_{ij} = a\}
g_{ij}
+
\mathbf{z}_i^{\mathsf{T}}\boldsymbol{\gamma}
+
\epsilon_i.
\]
Tractor-Mix extends this to related individuals by incorporating a random effect with covariance \(\mathbf{K}\).

### H.2 Cross-Ancestry Genetic Correlation

The bivariate LMM is
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
\mathbf{y}_1 \\
\mathbf{y}_2
\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
\mathbf{X}_1 & \mathbf{0} \\
\mathbf{0} & \mathbf{X}_2
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
\boldsymbol{\beta}_1 \\
\boldsymbol{\beta}_2
\end{pmatrix}
+
\begin{pmatrix}
\mathbf{g}_1 \\
\mathbf{g}_2
\end{pmatrix}
+
\begin{pmatrix}
\boldsymbol{\epsilon}_1 \\
\boldsymbol{\epsilon}_2
\end{pmatrix},
\]
with
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
\mathbf{g}_1 \\
\mathbf{g}_2
\end{pmatrix}
\sim
\mathcal{N}
\left(
\mathbf{0},
\begin{pmatrix}
\sigma_{g_1}^2 \mathbf{K}_1 &
\rho_g \sigma_{g_1}\sigma_{g_2} \mathbf{K}_{12} \\
\rho_g \sigma_{g_1}\sigma_{g_2} \mathbf{K}_{12}^{\mathsf{T}} &
\sigma_{g_2}^2 \mathbf{K}_2
\end{pmatrix}
\right).
\]

### H.3 Local Ancestry Estimation Error

Let \(I = \mathbf{1}\{a_{ij}=a\}\) be the true indicator and \(\hat{I} = \mathbf{1}\{\hat{a}_{ij}=a\}\) the estimated indicator. Suppose
\[
\text{sensitivity} = se = P(\hat{I}=1 \mid I=1),
\qquad
\text{specificity} = sp = P(\hat{I}=0 \mid I=0).
\]
Let \(p = P(I=1)\). Then
\[
E[\hat{I}] = p\, se + (1-p)(1-sp),
\]
\[
\operatorname{Var}(\hat{I}) = E[\hat{I}](1-E[\hat{I}]),
\]
\[
\operatorname{Cov}(\hat{I}, I) = p(1-p)(se + sp - 1).
\]
If the true model is \(y = \beta I + \epsilon\) and the fitted model uses \(\hat{I}\), the probability limit of the estimated coefficient is
\[
\hat{\beta}
\to
\beta
\frac{\operatorname{Cov}(\hat{I}, I)}{\operatorname{Var}(\hat{I})}
=
\beta
\frac{p(1-p)(se+sp-1)}
{E[\hat{I}](1-E[\hat{I}])}.
\]
Thus switch errors and phasing errors attenuate or reverse ancestry-specific effect estimates. In highly admixed samples, bias of \(10\)–\(30\%\) is plausible. Correction requires measurement-error models, multiple imputation over local ancestry, or simulation-based calibration.

